  "intro": "The U.S. exported **$283.61B** of machinery and mechanical equipment in 2025, up 12.4% on 2024 and 55% above 2020. The largest headings were computers (8471, $61.18B), computer parts (8473, $35.76B), semiconductor equipment (8486, $19.27B). Mexico and Canada took **41%** between them.\n\nTariffs are a minor factor for most buyers: Mexico, South Korea and the other free-trade partners charge nothing on qualifying goods, and the EU cut its remaining tariffs to zero on July 1, 2026. The exceptions are **Canada**, which since September 2026 surtaxes about 50 machinery tariff items at 15–50% regardless of CUSMA, and **China**, which adds a 10% extra duty (and another 10% on farm machinery). What usually decides access is conformity approval and, for machine tools, chip-making equipment and some pumps and valves, U.S. export-control licensing.",
  "latestYear": 2025,
  "priorYear": 2024,
